---
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE APPLIED, 16 September 2026 ─────────────────────────
//
// This page has been rewritten against commercial-pressure-cleaning-copy-rewrite.md
// (workbook row 29, primary `commercial pressure cleaning services` 150/mo,
// cluster 610/mo). Section numbers in the comments below are that brief's.
// Implemented: approved title and meta, new H1 with the old one demoted to
// the hero subheading line, walkways and forecourts honoured in the opening
// paragraph, the approved CTA in all four places plus as the closing lead-in
// sentence, a NEW industrial H2 carrying 280/mo the page captured none of,
// the renamed premises and FAQ headings, the widened premises intro, the
// narrowed comparison table and the full-footprint town list.
//
// NO BLOCKING CONFLICT on this page's CTA, unlike the carpet sibling. One
// workbook change request is open and non-blocking: the approved meta leads
// on a mapped secondary rather than the primary. See `pageMeta`.
//
// Not done here, because they are not copy: the URL mismatch (workbook wants
// /commercial-cleaning/commercial-pressure-cleaning, the build is flat — a
// redirect decision for the build team), the canonical domain, FAQ schema,
// the header's competing "Get an instant quote", and hiding the testimonial
// block until real quotes exist.
//
// Commercial pressure cleaning — a LEAF page under the /commercial-cleaning/
// hub (navigation.ts, "Commercial services" group). Sibling of
// /commercial-cleaning/commercial-carpet-cleaning/, built in the same pass and sharing its
// structure deliberately: two leaves off one hub that open differently read
// as two different sites.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTED WHEN THIS PAGE WAS BUILT. One does now, and it's
// named at the top of this file. The note below is the original build's.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS. Every other page in src/pages/ was built from a
// file in content-plans/; there is none for this one. All copy below is
// DRAFTED, not supplied, and needs sign-off before launch. Where a claim
// depends on a fact only TLB holds, it ships as a visible [bracket] per house
// convention rather than as a plausible guess.
//
// ⚠️ THIS PAGE HAS A RESIDENTIAL TWIN. /house-cleaning/pressure-cleaning/ sits under
// the Home Cleaning mega-menu ("Outside your home") and does not exist yet.
// The two will compete for the same queries unless they stay in their lanes:
// THIS page is workplaces, WHS, trading hours and wash water compliance;
// THAT page is driveways, paths, fences and house exteriors for a homeowner.
// Whoever builds the residential one should read this comment first.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH:
//   - IS THIS DELIVERED IN HOUSE? Pressure cleaning needs its own plant
//     (petrol unit, surface cleaner, water tank, in many cases a hot water
//     machine) and nothing in the brand foundation document or any existing
//     page says TLB owns any of it. If the work is subcontracted, the
//     comparison table below (row one: "employed by TLB, not subcontracted")
//     contradicts the page it sits on and must come off this page. See §10.
//   - HOT WATER OR COLD? The scope block below says grease and oil need hot
//     water or a degreaser with dwell time. If TLB runs cold water only, say
//     so plainly and drop the grease claims, because a cold unit will not
//     shift a loading dock and the client will see that on the day.
//   - WATER CAPTURE. Non-branded FAQ 4 states that wash water carrying oil,
//     grease or detergent must not enter stormwater. That is correct and it
//     is the single biggest liability on this page. Confirm what TLB actually
//     does about capture and disposal BEFORE this publishes, because the page
//     currently tells the reader to ask every cleaner a question TLB has not
//     answered about itself.
//   - Water supply, access and pricing basis are bracketed. All three come up
//     on the first phone call.
//   - [insured] and [police-checked] in card 3 are inherited from the hub,
//     where the same brackets are already open. One answer fixes both pages.
//
// PHOTOGRAPHY: five generated assets of this page's own, in
// src/assets/commercial_cleaning/pressure/, with their prompts in the
// IMAGE-PROMPTS.md beside them. Nothing is reused from the hub's folder,
// because all four frames there are indoor office scenes. One deliberate
// deviation from IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 runs through the three people shots:
// the apron is dropped for outdoor work. It is argued at the §1 hero comment
// and it is TLB's call to reverse.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// Every asset on this page is its own. Nothing was reused from the hub's
// folder: all four frames there are indoor office scenes and none of them
// honestly stands in for outdoor hard-surface work, which is the one thing
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §5 rules out ("never relabel an existing photo to mean
// something it doesn't show"). Prompts are in
// src/assets/commercial_cleaning/pressure/IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim. This page does NOT borrow the
// homepage's indoor frame the way the hub and the carpet sibling do — an
// interior behind an outdoor page's copy was a compromise, so a wide exterior
// was generated for it instead. Deliberately empty of people and equipment:
// it sits behind the trust bar and the definition paragraph, so it is
// atmosphere, and the generous clean paving across the lower third is what
// the copy sits over.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns, serviceComparisonRows } from '../../data/comparison';
import { footprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { localBusiness } from '../../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. Focus keyword leads the title, matching the hub's pattern.
// "Commercial pressure cleaning" is the menu label and the primary term;
// "pressure washing" is the common synonym and is carried in body copy
// rather than in the title, so the two do not read as different services.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on "Commercial Pressure Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning":
// Approved title and meta, pasted verbatim from the brief (workbook row 29)
// per its Rule 3. "Services" is what turns the head term into the mapped
// primary `commercial pressure cleaning services` (150/mo, Keyword Mapping
// & Metadata 15 Sep 2026).
// In `pageMeta`, on `description`:
// 141 characters. The live one was 161 and would have been truncated.
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST, brief §3: this leads on `commercial high pressure
// cleaning`, a mapped SECONDARY at 100/mo, rather than the mapped primary
// at 150/mo. Defensible — the two share most of their words and the
// secondary reads better in a sentence — but it means the meta component
// cannot score full marks on the primary. Raised for a decision rather
// than silently fixed.
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §7 The primary CTA, defined once and repeated in four places per BFD §9.5:
// the hero button, pathway card 1, the button after the six stages and the
// closing block.
//
// The approved CTA is "Get a quote for your site today" — seven words, which
// is a sentence rather than a button label. So the full text lands in the
// closing block's lead-in line and the button takes its imperative core.
// Both come from the same approved text, per the brief's Rule 4, and the
// words stay on the page either way.
//
// NO BLOCKING CONFLICT HERE, unlike the carpet sibling. This page says a
// walkthrough produces a written scope and a price, and the approved CTA asks
// for a quote. Nothing promises a price sight unseen, which is what BFD
// §13.2 rules out.
// Brief §7. The button was one word short of the approved workbook CTA,
// `Get a quote for your site today`, while the closing block's lead-in
// already ended on the full sentence — so the page was running two slightly
// different versions of the same CTA in two places. At 31 characters the full
// version still fits a button. One primary CTA, repeated, per BFD 9.5.
//
// No BFD 13.2 conflict on this page: there is no `Get an instant quote`
// anywhere on it, and the pricing block promises a walkthrough and a written
// scope, which is correct for condition-priced work.

// In `business`, on `areaServed`:
// ⚠️ NSW only, following the hub's call and this page's meta description.
// It matters more here than elsewhere: the wash water rules cited in
// non-branded FAQ 4 are NSW law, and QLD's equivalents sit under different
// legislation. If this service crosses the border, that answer needs a QLD
// clause before it publishes.

// §1 Hero. Kicker carries the focus keyword so a visitor from a search result
// gets confirmation above the H1, which is free to be a benefit. Same call as
// the hub and the carpet sibling.
//
// CTA is "Book a site walkthrough", not the sitewide "Get an instant quote".
// Nobody can price a car park from a form: it turns on area, surface,
// staining, access to water and power, and whether the wash water has to be
// captured. Branded FAQ 5 says so.
//
// A SCENE PHOTO, family A, following the call the hub made at TLB's
// direction: a studio cut-out on a plain background says "a cleaner" without
// saying commercial. Here the building, the forecourt and the petrol unit are
// what carry it, and none of them survives a cut-out.
//
// Two consequences, same as the hub's foyer hero:
//   - `imageFit` is UNSET on the render below, taking Placeholder's `cover`
//     default. No transparency to preserve, no figure to stand on the edge.
//   - `ratio` is 1/1 because the SOURCE is 1024x1024, so a box matching the
//     file crops nothing. Regenerate square if this is ever reshot, or change
//     both together.
//
// ⚠️ UNIFORM DEVIATION, made deliberately and repeated across all three of
// this page's people shots. IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 fixes the canonical uniform
// as aqua polo, dark teal apron and teal gloves, and §3 asks for it verbatim
// in every prompt. The APRON IS DROPPED here, replaced with dark teal work
// trousers and black boots, because an apron on a wet outdoor high-pressure
// job reads as fancy dress and undercuts the competence the page is selling.
// Polo, gloves and palette are unchanged. If TLB would rather hold the
// uniform absolutely constant sitewide, these three regenerate with the
// apron; the call is theirs, and it is flagged rather than made silently.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. An AI image of a person who does not exist.
// Honest as generic service imagery and NOT presented as a named team member,
// which IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 forbids. Treat as a good placeholder.
//
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The exact phrase is worth 15 points scored present or absent,
// and naming the three surfaces is the specificity BFD §14 asks for rather
// than decoration.
// In `hero`, on `subheading`:
// Brief §5. Rule 5's second slot, which this page did not have. An earlier
// note here said Hero had no subheading prop; it does — Hero.astro renders
// `subheading` directly beneath the <h1> and above the lead, outside the
// heading, which is exactly where this belongs.
//
// Its extension is the TIMING, distinct from the H1's scope. The two
// extensions share no words, so this is the phrase a second time rather
// than the line a second time. Out-of-hours scheduling is what the approved
// meta leads on and what booking FAQ 1 answers.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §6. The old paragraph repeated the H1's exact phrase and almost its
// exact extension one line below it, which read as the headline printed
// twice. The new subheading now carries the second instance with a
// different extension, so the paragraph drops the phrase and keeps the
// argument. "Your building is judged from the footpath" stays — it is the
// best opening line on any of the commercial pages.

// §2 Definition. The answer-target block for the focus keyword and the first
// place the phrase appears in body copy. Paragraph two does the synonym work
// ("pressure washing", "high pressure cleaning") and, more usefully,
// introduces soft washing, which is the distinction most competitor pages in
// this category get wrong.
//
// In `definitionBody`, on paragraph 1:
// Brief §10. The opening paragraph is worth 10 of the 100 primary-keyword
// points and was the only one of the eight components this page failed,
// because the phrase appeared here without the word "services". Two words
// fix it, and the fourth sentence keeps `commercial pressure cleaning` as a
// variant so nothing is lost.

// §3 Pathway cards. The three questions a commercial buyer arrives with, in
// their words. Same framing as the hub and the carpet sibling, re-pointed at
// hard surfaces.
//
// All three images are family A scene photos, generated for these slots.
// Card 1 is the only one with no person in it, on purpose: the subject is the
// surface, and a figure would compete with the line down the middle of it.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on "A concrete car park halfway through being pressure cleaned, weathered grey and stained on one side of a straight line and pale and clean on the other":
// ⚠️ ONE FRAME of work in progress, not a before/after pair implying
// a specific job, which IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 rules out. The line is
// the width of a rotary surface cleaner, which is the same tool the
// scope block below names.
// In `pathwayCards`, on "A cleaner in uniform standing beside her pressure washer on a commercial forecourt, turning to the camera with a friendly expression":
// A visible, friendly face, for the same reason the hub's card 3
// carries one: the copy beside it promises "the same small team you
// have met". A figure turned away would argue against its own caption.
// In `pathwayCards`, on "I want to know who is on my site" (description):
// ⚠️ Two brackets inherited from the hub. Same answer fixes both.
// "Site" is used here rather than "your place" on purpose: BFD 2.3
// reserves the warmer wording for homes, and this reader is
// responsible for premises, not living in them.

// §4 Scope of work. THE MOST IMPORTANT BLOCK ON THIS PAGE, for the same
// reason it is on the carpet sibling: commercial-cleaning.astro flags in its
// own source that the site tells readers to demand a written scope four times
// and never shows one.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Confirm each one, and cut anything TLB does not actually do. Two carry
// their own blockers, marked inline.
//
// The order is the order of the job, and the last two exist because they are
// what separates a contractor from someone with a hired unit from Bunnings.
//
// In `scopeItems`, on "Pre-treat, then clean" (description):
// ⚠️ BLOCKER. Hot water is named here. Confirm TLB has a hot unit; if
// it is cold water only, cut the hot water clause and soften the grease
// claims across the page, because a cold machine will not shift a
// loading dock.
// In `scopeItems`, on "Wash water managed, not just hosed away" (description):
// ⚠️ BLOCKER, and the most serious one on the page. See non-branded FAQ
// 4 and the file header. This card describes correct practice, NOT a
// verified description of what TLB does today.

// §5 "Worth knowing". Same job as the hub's Award block and the carpet
// sibling's warranty block: make the reader distrust the cheapest quote
// without making a single claim about TLB. Here the lever is that pressure
// cleaning is the one trade where the cheap operator can do permanent damage
// in about four seconds, and the second is the legal one.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFY BEFORE LAUNCH. The stormwater point is a statement about the law
// and it is correct in substance for NSW: under the Protection of the
// Environment Operations Act 1997 (NSW), allowing polluting matter to enter
// stormwater is an offence, and councils and the EPA both enforce it. It is
// worded here as a duty on the site rather than as legal advice, and it names
// no section number and no penalty amount on purpose, because those change.
// Have someone confirm the phrasing, and consider linking NSW EPA or the
// relevant council directly (BFD 3.3 asks for authoritative sources).

// §18 NEW SECTION. Industrial sites as a distinct case.
//
// THE HEADLINE FINDING ON THIS PAGE. Three of the six mapped secondaries are
// industrial terms — `industrial high pressure cleaning` 100/mo, `industrial
// pressure cleaning near me` 100/mo and `high pressure industrial cleaning`
// 80/mo, 280/mo combined (Keyword Mapping & Metadata, 15 Sep 2026). That is
// more monthly volume than the mapped primary carries on its own, and the
// live page captured NONE of it: the word "industrial" did not appear once in
// body copy, while the navigation lists Warehouses and industrial sites,
// Factories and Breweries as served premises.
//
// The gap is competitive as well as arithmetic. On the live SERP (16 Sep
// 2026) the strongest local pressure cleaning competitor runs a dedicated
// commercial and industrial sites page.
//
// Placed after the persuasion block so the buying argument comes first, and
// before the premises list so it leads into it.
//
// ⚠️ The bracket in paragraph three is NOT a new ask. It is the same
// confirmation already outstanding in stage six of §14 — TLB's wash water
// capture method and disposal arrangement — which is the single most
// expensive unfilled placeholder on this page.

// §6 Related pages. Not the hub's full fifteen-pill premises list, which would
// compete with the hub for the same links. These are the settings where
// outdoor hard surfaces are the actual problem, plus the routes back up the
// cluster and across to the carpet sibling.
//
// ✅ Every href below is confirmed and nests under /commercial-cleaning/.
// The client's URL sheet of 17 Sep 2026 gives the slugs for every Commercial
// row it lists; factories and breweries were settled separately the same day,
// to the sheet's own convention. See premises.ts.
//
// ⚠️ /house-cleaning/pressure-cleaning/ is the RESIDENTIAL twin (Home Cleaning menu,
// "Outside your home") and is deliberately linked from here, so a homeowner
// who lands on the commercial page has somewhere to go rather than enquiring
// for the wrong service. The label says "for homes" for the same reason.

// §10 Comparison table. The shared rows from src/data/comparison.ts, now on a
// fifth page.
//
// ⚠️ CONDITIONAL, and this is the file header's first launch blocker. Row one
// is "Nine full-time cleaners employed by TLB, not subcontracted". If
// pressure cleaning is subcontracted to an operator with the plant, this
// table contradicts the page it is sitting on and has to come off this page.
// A question for TLB, not a rewrite.
//
// In `comparisonTable`, on `rows`:
// Brief §19, scorecard finding 7. "Who cleans your home" becomes "your
// site" on a commercial page, and the holiday-lets row comes off: a strata
// committee or a facilities manager reading it here is being shown a table
// written for somebody else. Every other row is unchanged. See
// data/comparison.ts.

// §11 Branded FAQs. Written in the CUSTOMER'S voice, where "we" means the
// reader's business rather than TLB. That register is the hub's and it is
// held across all three commercial pages.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "What do you need from us on the day?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKET. Access to a tap and a power point is the ordinary
// arrangement, but whether TLB carries its own water tank and generator
// changes the answer completely, and nobody has said. This is the third
// question every site asks on the first call.

// §12 Non-branded FAQs. The long-tail block, aimed at what people search
// rather than what they ask us.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and it is heavier than the carpet page's:
//   - Soft washing vs high pressure, and which surfaces take which. Standard
//     industry practice, correct as stated.
//   - Wash water to stormwater. Correct for NSW under the Protection of the
//     Environment Operations Act 1997. Stated as a duty, with no section
//     number and no penalty figure, both of which date. ⚠️ SILENT ON QLD,
//     which matters if this service crosses the border. Councils also set
//     their own trade waste conditions for discharge to sewer, so "check with
//     your council" is doing real work in that answer, not hedging.
//   - Slip hazard and exclusion zones. A genuine WHS duty on the site
//     occupier under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (NSW), phrased here
//     without naming it.
//   - Frequency ("twice a year for most sites") is an industry observation,
//     not a rule, and is hedged accordingly.
// Recommended before launch: a read by someone who prices exterior work, and
// a visible "last reviewed" date on this block.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does commercial pressure cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same call as the carpet page. This is the
// most searched question on the topic and the page cannot answer it
// without a number from TLB. A "from $X per square metre" that turns
// out to be wrong is worse than no number, and the ACCC treats
// from-pricing as a claim.

// §13 Closing CTA. Same offer as the hero, worded as what actually happens.
// The closing lead ends on the full approved CTA sentence, which used to be
// a separate constant (`primaryCtaSentence`). It is now part of that lead in
// the content file.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/commercial-pressure-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Commercial pressure cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/commercial-pressure-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of `primaryCta` (§7 above). It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    // A card with no button of its own gets the page's main button.
    pathways: section.pathwayCards.extend({
      cards: z.array(section.pathwayCards.shape.cards.element.extend({ cta: section.optional(section.link) })).min(1),
    }),
    scope: section.iconGrid,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    industrial: section.textBlock,
    // This page's own short list of settings, not the shared premises list.
    settings: section.tagCloud.extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    // The columns and rows are the shared table in Site settings. The page
    // owns its framing and the label of the "who cleans" row.
    comparison: section.comparisonTable
      .pick({ heading: true, lead: true, cornerLabel: true })
      .extend({ whoCleansLabel: section.text }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared footprint line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/commercial-pressure-cleaning/',
};

const primaryCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  // ⚠️ NSW only. See the note "In `business`" above: the wash water answer
  // cites NSW law.
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers and Tweed, NSW',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '1/1');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
  cta: card.cta ?? primaryCta,
}));
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? primaryCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid across
        both sections. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026. Measured over this
        frame's own darkest region under the copy column, 75% gave 4.53:1 — a
        bare pass with no margin over a photo. 90% gives 6.12:1.
        Backdrop.astro's `opacity` doc carries the method.

        The photo is this page's own wide exterior rather than the homepage
        interior the hub and the carpet sibling borrow. See the import. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={page.pathways.heading} headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.scope.heading}
      lead={page.scope.lead}
      note={page.scope.note}
      items={page.scope.items}
      cta={primaryCta}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* §18. New H2, per the brief. `light` between the dark "two things to
        ask" band above and TagCloud's brand fill below, so the page keeps
        alternating rather than stacking three filled bands. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading={page.industrial.heading}
      body={page.industrial.body}
      cta={page.industrial.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.industrial.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* ⚠️ RESERVED SLOT: before and after slider (BeforeAfter.astro).

        A half-cleaned car park is the most persuasive image in this entire
        category, and the component is already running on five pages.

        NOT BUILT because BeforeAfter has no reserved-slot mode by design: its
        `src` is required, since a comparison slider with nothing to compare
        has no meaningful empty state. No commercial exterior photography
        exists.

        THE PHOTO BRIEF: one surface, shot from a fixed tripod position,
        before and after, with NOTHING moved between the frames — no car
        parked in the second shot, no bin shifted. Concrete and pavers are the
        best subjects because the change is unmistakable. Two or three pairs
        is plenty. Drop it in here, between "Worth knowing" and the related
        pages. */}

